NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.320
Cool.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.700
So maybe first we could talk
about what it is you migrated.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.030
So I know that you
performed this migration,

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:29.900
I think it was either
late 2016 or early 2017.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.760
So maybe you could walk us
through your environment

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.110
that you had to migrate,
some of the challenges

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:39.639
that you encountered, and how
the overall experience went.

00:03:39.639 --> 00:03:40.430
DAVID MYTTON: Sure.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:47.510
So there's two key problems that
have been keeping us working

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.360
hard on the technical challenges
over the last eight years

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:50.860
or so.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.750
And the first of those is
processing large volumes

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:57.890
of incoming data in near as
close as possible to real time

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.770
so that we can trigger
alerts for customers.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.470
And then the second
problem is the storage

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:06.470
of large volumes of
time series data.

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:10.170
And that's what powers the
graphs for our customers.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:11.870
So the two use cases
obviously getting

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:13.700
notified when
something is broken.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.100
But once you've got
that notification,

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.160
you need to be able to
see what's going on.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.769
And so the graphs are
pretty important to any kind

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:23.840
of monitoring product.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.810
Over the years we've
scaled [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:28.850
been using the product.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:32.990
And having a stable
and high performance

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:37.080
time series database has
been a big challenge for us.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:40.460
And we've also had the challenge
that at the time that we were

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:43.330
scaling there was
no real product that

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:46.654
could solve the different
scaling challenges,

00:04:46.654 --> 00:04:48.320
either as an open
source product that we

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.940
would have to run ourselves
or as a managed service.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:55.160
And so through the years
we've built our own things

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:56.170
internally.

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:58.470
And then as services
have become available,

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.140
we've looked at how
we can replace them

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.510
with something that's managed
for us, because we're just

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:04.670
a small team of engineers.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:11.150
So this brought us, at the
end of 2016 or in 2016,

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.470
where we were having problems
with our existing time series

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:16.070
database.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:18.860
And we'd built a custom
version, a custom

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.060
system on top of MongoDB.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.330
And it's a very good
general purpose database

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:28.580
and has over the years and has
scaled with us, with problems,

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:30.650
but problems that we've
been able to solve.

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:34.130
But we found that we were
spending a lot of time building

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:34.910
custom code.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.780
So time series databases is
a very specialist application

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:42.115
and we built our own custom
version on top of Mongo.

00:05:42.115 --> 00:05:45.460
And so we had to maintain
that and build new features

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.320
and keep it running,
but also scaling

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.190
the hardware side of things.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:53.270
And we were hosted
on bare metal servers

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:56.550
and also across a
virtualized environment.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:58.160
And we were spending
a lot of money

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.860
and a lot of time
managing what had

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:04.070
to be some pretty hefty
servers, a lot of SSD storage,

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:05.660
a lot of RAM.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.270
And we got to the point where
we were spending so much time--

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.280
and also cache on
the hardware itself--

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.620
that we decided to evaluate
the options of replacing

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.420
the custom built time
series database with either

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:20.040
a product or a managed service.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.290
So we looked at as many
different options as we could.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.040
We looked at products
from Amazon and Redshift

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:26.480
and also RDS.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.830
We also looked at open source.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:31.940
So Cassandra, MySQL, building
another custom version

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:35.600
on top of that, as many
companies have done,

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:38.800
rewriting it in the latest
version of Mongo DB.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:43.670
Or, as we came to use, we also
looked at Google Cloud Big

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:48.500
Table, which was very early
on, but was looking promising,

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:52.280
particularly because it
had an official connector

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:57.490
for OpenTSDB, which is an open
source time series database.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.120
That's what the name is.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:03.950
And that meant that we could
offload a lot of the custom

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:07.120
feature development to
an open source product,

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.020
so building things
like aggregating data,

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.920
mathematical functions,
dealing with retention

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:15.260
of data, that was all part
of the standard feature

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:18.380
set of OpenTSDB.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:22.910
And then the management
of the actual data

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.810
store behind the
scenes, and that

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.870
could be handled by
Google as a Cloud service,

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:29.420
because we could just
connect the two together.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.760
And so our migration
over to Google Cloud

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:35.210
started with a single
service, OpenTSDB,

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:37.430
on top of Google
Cloud Big Table,

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:39.830
as the most expensive
and most time consuming

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:42.470
service that we had to run.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.050
And then as we completed
that migration,

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:50.960
that went live in just over a
year ago, February last year.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.750
And having successfully
migrated that running

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:56.420
across two different
Cloud environments,

00:07:56.420 --> 00:08:00.980
we then decided that we would
migrate our full workload over

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:04.160
to Google Cloud, using
Container Engine, Compute

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:06.980
Engine, and a couple other
different other products, as

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:08.160
well.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.056
So that's a quick run through
of the migration story.

00:08:12.056 --> 00:08:14.130
NICK ORLOVE: No, that's
really interesting.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:16.065
And I feel like you're
coming from a position

00:08:16.065 --> 00:08:17.990
that a lot of our
customers are trying

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:20.885
to grapple with is they have
some custom built applications

00:08:20.885 --> 00:08:23.093
that are running on premise
that they really see some

00:08:23.093 --> 00:08:24.590
of the value in,
and that might be

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:26.850
their competitive
differentiation.

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:28.250
But then they also
have some kind

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:31.091
of more generic
applications that

00:08:31.091 --> 00:08:32.299
really could be run anywhere.

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:35.990
And they're trying to identify,
hey, do we do a lift and shift

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:38.360
or do we try to rewrite
some of our applications?

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:41.600
And I know that a
lot of our customers

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:43.480
try to grapple with that.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.530
But it sounds like you
found a really good medium

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:49.010
of identifying your strengths,
Server Density's strengths,

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.200
as well as Google's
strengths, and then

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:54.270
just being able to kind of
move over what you could

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:57.650
and then optimize
everything else in terms

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:01.550
of making it Cloud native
before moving it over.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:05.160
So since you've already
kind of crossed this bridge,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:06.792
and since I'm sure
some of our viewers

00:09:06.792 --> 00:09:08.750
are trying to debate if
they are ready for that

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:14.291
crossing themselves,
how about any surprises

00:09:14.291 --> 00:09:15.290
that you've encountered.

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:20.330
And let's keep it unbiased,
maybe positive surprises

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:22.220
but then also maybe
some negative surprises

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:25.769
that you wish you had known
before you did the move.

00:09:25.769 --> 00:09:27.060
DAVID MYTTON: Yeah, absolutely.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:30.690
So I think the one that I
focused in on my blog post

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:32.680
is the cost side of things.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:34.340
I think that's
really interesting.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:38.520
Because people often made the
comparison between co-location,

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.370
buying their own
hardware, and actually

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:43.050
physically racking it,
through to very, very

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:46.910
cheap dedicated servers, all
the way to Cloud environments.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.580
And they're comparable in a way.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:53.300
But really, it's not completely
appropriate to compare

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:54.080
like for like.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.240
They're not the
same kind of thing.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:00.890
In most cases, if you're
just comparing CPU and memory

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:03.140
in the Cloud to what
you could buy hardware,

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:07.640
the Cloud is always going
to be more expensive.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.770
You don't really
get the cost savings

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:11.900
just on the pure dollar
value of the resources

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:12.860
that you're buying.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:16.040
The savings that you get
are in the efficiencies

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.010
of moving a core
workload to the Cloud

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:21.530
so that you no longer
have to manage it.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:25.850
And this comes down to one
of the huge advantages of Big

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:30.360
Table, in particular, in
that we can get scalability

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.660
and we know that
it's going to cost us

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:36.320
a very specific amount of money,
and it's completely linear.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:40.090
And we can do that the click of
a button in the Cloud console

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:41.480
or through the API.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:44.570
And that makes
the predictability

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:47.600
and the management
very, very easy.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.000
Just comparing to
hardware is very difficult

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.050
to do because all of the
added benefits that you get

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:55.920
are quite difficult
to calculate.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.490
So the cost of your team,
the software updates,

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:02.420
the management behind the
scenes, the cost side of things

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:04.000
can be difficult.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.310
But with that caveat and that
you can't really compare them

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:10.580
directly, there are some
interesting features

00:11:10.580 --> 00:11:13.730
of the Cloud which are
certainly worth highlighting.

00:11:13.730 --> 00:11:16.670
And in particular, it's
that granularity of cost

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:17.600
that you get.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.610
So when you buy a
dedicated server,

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.912
or if you buy a physical
server and rack it yourself,

00:11:22.912 --> 00:11:25.370
you know that it's probably
just going to be a single cost.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.328
There's going to be the
monthly fee that you're

00:11:27.328 --> 00:11:29.870
paying for that server
or the one-time fee

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:34.530
that you pay for the hardware
itself plus the rack itself.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:37.700
In the Cloud, absolutely
everything that you use,

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:40.535
every single resource
is billed individually

00:11:40.535 --> 00:11:43.350
at precisely the amount
that you're paying for.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:45.320
And this seems like
an obvious thing.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:49.010
But it is a shift in
thinking when you're

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:52.190
used to just buying
a server and paying

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:55.760
a fixed amount every month,
regardless of the usage

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:57.590
and whether you're
maxing out the server

00:11:57.590 --> 00:11:59.090
or whether you're not.

00:11:59.090 --> 00:12:00.830
Whereas in the Cloud,
you're actually

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:03.890
paying precisely for
the amount of resources

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:05.540
that you are using.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:09.230
And this can be quite
difficult to predict.

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:11.630
And there are a
number of instances

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:14.960
where you can get
sudden spikes of usage

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:18.170
that you just weren't expecting.

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:21.360
So for example,
if you're looking

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:23.680
at your current performance,
so buying storage,

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:25.360
for example, and
with Cloud disks

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:30.315
you're often a buying certain
number of IOPS, the operations.

00:12:30.315 --> 00:12:32.550
And it's actually
quite difficult

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:34.020
to measure your
current performance

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:37.050
to predict exactly how
your workload is going

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:41.090
to migrate over from physical
spinning disks or SSDs

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:43.220
over to IOPS in the Cloud.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.150
And it's off of that
IOPS number is often

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:49.040
linked to the storage volume
size, at least on Google Cloud,

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:50.940
but also the number of CPUs.

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:55.742
And that is unusual when you are
just buying a dedicated server.

00:12:55.742 --> 00:12:57.450
We might not even know
the number of IOPs

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:58.408
that you're purchasing.

00:12:58.408 --> 00:13:01.260
You just know that with an
SSD, it's going to be fast.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:04.560
And that has the side
effect of some time

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.360
you're oversizing
your instances,

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:08.970
because you might not
need the number of CPUs

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:10.470
that you're buying.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:13.140
And you might not need the full
volume size that you're buying,

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:15.810
but you need to buy a
certain number of IOPs.

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:20.310
So that's one of the, I
suppose, the downsides

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:24.335
in the pros and cons of that.

00:13:24.335 --> 00:13:26.600
NICK ORLOVE: That's
really interesting.

00:13:26.600 --> 00:13:29.710
So I like how you called out
these unexpected costs, things

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:31.610
that you didn't
really anticipate.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:34.150
So you mentioned the
over provisioning

00:13:34.150 --> 00:13:39.790
and how to accurately monitor or
identify how much of a resource

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:41.260
you actually require.

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:43.970
But I'm actually
sharing my screen.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.276
Let me just jump over
into your blog post.

00:13:46.276 --> 00:13:48.650
YURI: You have to hit the
Present button, I think, first.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:49.270
NICK ORLOVE: Oh.

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:49.936
Actually, I did.

00:13:49.936 --> 00:13:50.450
YURI: OK.

00:13:50.450 --> 00:13:50.900
Great.

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:52.275
NICK ORLOVE: So
one of the things

00:13:52.275 --> 00:13:55.720
that I wanted to call out was
kind of the networking cost.

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:59.390
So you mentioned that everything
in the Cloud is billed

00:13:59.390 --> 00:14:03.370
and it's billed precisely,
which makes sense.

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:05.530
But in your blog
post, you mentioned

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:08.630
that there was a period of time
where all of the sudden you

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:13.300
saw a huge spike in traffic
exiting your network, which

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:17.080
ended up costing you
some additional funds

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:19.632
through Google.

00:14:19.632 --> 00:14:22.720
And what I like is
that you and Google

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:26.290
were able to work together and
identify that it was fraud,

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:27.780
at the end of the day.

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:29.500
However, would you
mind taking a second

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:34.420
and just explaining your
process of identifying that

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:38.440
and how you were able to
work with Google on getting

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:40.000
that resolved?

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:40.960
DAVID MYTTON: Yeah.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.160
So that's a really
interesting example,

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:47.210
because it's a good example of
abuse or an unexpected spike.

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:49.360
And you either got
it as actual abuse

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:51.520
that someone is
misusing your service

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:54.310
or it's a spike of traffic
because you are suddenly,

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:57.850
unexpectedly popular
on Reddit or on Twitter

00:14:57.850 --> 00:14:58.977
or something like that.

00:14:58.977 --> 00:15:01.060
And there are similar use
cases, although probably

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:03.370
the second one is the
one you want, as opposed

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:05.770
to the abuse use case.

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:10.570
So in this instance, we had
a large spike in traffic

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:13.595
to a Firebase
website that we host

00:15:13.595 --> 00:15:16.380
in Firebase just as
a static site, so

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:19.790
at the serverdensity.com
website itself.

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:23.530
And we have a number
of budget alerts set up

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:25.990
on the different projects
within Google Cloud.

00:15:25.990 --> 00:15:28.990
So we know what
our expected budget

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:30.370
is going to be for a month.

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:34.750
And we have alerts set up so
that as we're reaching it,

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:37.837
the percentage usage should
basically match how far along

00:15:37.837 --> 00:15:38.670
we are in the month.

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:43.720
So 15th of January, thereabout,
should be 50% usage.

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:46.800
And so we have this sudden
spike and our alerts

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:51.430
triggered saying that we'd used
much more of the monthly budget

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:53.080
than we'd been expecting.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.720
And digging into
the usage graphs,

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.930
we saw that massive
spike that you showed,

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:01.450
which is basically the baseline.

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:03.670
Our usage of the
website was completely

00:16:03.670 --> 00:16:05.710
at the bottom of the
axis on the graph,

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:08.830
so we couldn't even
see our actual usage

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:11.280
because that massive
spike went all the way up.

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:14.950
And so, that was simply a case
of opening a ticket with Google

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:19.480
Support and saying, this looks
like an unusual traffic spike,

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:22.780
not least because it
spiked for a couple of days

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:24.730
and then went back down again.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:28.700
And working with Google
Support get into the logs.

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:30.180
It was very, very simple.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:33.300
They just simply said, this
looks like abusive traffic

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:35.670
and we're going to
credit you for it.

00:16:35.670 --> 00:16:40.210
And so I think just actually
raising the issues first

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:44.030
with Google makes sense to do,
but having the budget alerts

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.580
and the data and the
monitoring is important

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:49.788
so you can spot these problems.

00:16:49.788 --> 00:16:53.035
NICK ORLOVE: That actually
absolutely make sense.

00:16:53.035 --> 00:16:55.880
And so you mentioned
keeping an eye

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:59.244
on the budgeting and monitoring
of your existing resources.

00:16:59.244 --> 00:17:01.160
Would you mind talking
for a second about what

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.669
you're using to do that?

00:17:03.669 --> 00:17:04.460
DAVID MYTTON: Sure.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:07.790
So just out of the box,
there are budget alerts

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:10.040
available on Google Cloud.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:12.780
And you can set this up
based on dollar amounts

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:16.004
and also comparisons
to your previous bill.

00:17:16.004 --> 00:17:18.349
So you can get alerts sent
out at different stages

00:17:18.349 --> 00:17:19.540
throughout the month.

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:22.400
And of course, the
unlimited flexibility

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:24.710
is kind of the whole
point of the Cloud.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:28.280
But it does mean that you can
have denial of service attacks

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:30.560
on your credit card
now, as opposed to just

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.420
your application.

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:33.920
So it's really
important to do that.

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:35.990
Because as far as
Google is concerned

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:39.560
this is just your application
running and it's not something

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:42.530
that's necessarily proactively
monitored by Google.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:45.770
And you may end up hitting
your credit card limits

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:48.660
and having a problem at
the end of the month.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:50.780
So it's important that
you do keep an eye on it.

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:53.180
So those budget
alerts are important.

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:58.280
But you can also get the
usage data dumped into CSV

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:02.450
and also exported into Google's
BigQuery product, which

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:06.230
is a great way to run analysis
on the raw data, what's

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:08.760
actually happening.

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:11.540
So you can analyze that
data obviously by yourself.

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:16.320
You can just pass the CSVs
or query BigQuery, even.

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:19.610
You can also use the
Data Studio product,

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:23.420
which is free as part of either
the G Suites or Google Cloud,

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:27.459
as part of the Google tools,
which does a decent job.

00:18:27.459 --> 00:18:29.000
But unfortunately,
it doesn't support

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:32.660
the discounts that are
available on Compute Engine.

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:36.440
So we found the
automatic sustained usage

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:38.900
discounts and the
committed usage discounts

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:40.970
don't get plotted correctly.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:43.700
And so we're using a
third party tool now

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:49.060
called reOptimize, which
basically queries BigQuery

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:51.390
and visualizes the data for you.

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.810
So we can keep an eye
on a almost real time--

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:56.700
it's not quite real time data--

00:18:56.700 --> 00:19:00.780
but it's updated within a couple
of hours, up to 24 hours later.

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:02.840
So we can keep an eye
on everything that's

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:04.760
happening in our account.

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:07.190
We can drill down into projects.

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:08.690
We can drill down
into the products.

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:12.380
And you can also label the
resources on the Google side,

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:14.960
so you can categorize and
group all the different things

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:18.129
that you're using and then
build reports based on that.

00:19:18.129 --> 00:19:19.170
NICK ORLOVE: Interesting.

00:19:19.170 --> 00:19:21.800
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:25.270
So in your blog post, you
also called out the need

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:26.590
to search for discounts.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:32.980
So you mentioned your customer
usage or committed usage

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:34.310
and sustained usage discounts.

00:19:34.310 --> 00:19:37.270
But would you mind
just kind of walking

00:19:37.270 --> 00:19:39.610
through what those are
and what kind of discounts

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:42.032
that you really look for?

00:19:42.032 --> 00:19:42.830
DAVID MYTTON: Sure.

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:45.670
So the sustained usage
discounts are, I think,

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:48.040
one of the best features
of Compute Engine when

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:51.340
compared to Amazon's
EC2, because you

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:54.370
don't have to do anything to get
the first level of discounts.

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:57.410
Just the fact that you're
using Compute Engine

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.110
throughout the month
means that you'll

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:01.210
get automatic
discounts if you use it

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:03.940
for a certain period of time.

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:07.300
This is in comparison to
Amazon's reserved instances,

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:10.210
which are certainly
very flexible,

00:20:10.210 --> 00:20:12.110
but they're so complicated
in figuring out

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:16.150
how many you need to buy,
for which instance types

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:18.440
and in different regions.

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.890
And so with the sustained
usage discounts,

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:23.680
they just apply automatically
and you get a discount

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:25.570
as you continue to
use the Compute Engine

00:20:25.570 --> 00:20:27.940
instance throughout the month.

00:20:27.940 --> 00:20:32.440
That's really good for usage
that you might not be expecting

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:34.700
or is part of a new
application, and not

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:37.190
something that you are
just learning about.

00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:39.880
But once you know what your
workload is going to be

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:42.700
and you've got static
workloads or workloads that you

00:20:42.700 --> 00:20:45.070
can predict into
the future, then you

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:48.850
can buy the committed usage
discounts, which gives you

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:50.910
even more of a discount.

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:52.750
You don't have to
pay anything up front

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:55.960
but you do get a commitment.

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:59.250
It's basically contracting for a
period of time, a year or three

00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:01.030
years, I think it is.

00:21:01.030 --> 00:21:02.710
YURI: So thank you
very much for that.

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:05.560
I wanted to give some of
the folks on our live chat

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:07.890
to get their questions answered.

00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:10.570
And now might be a good time
to ask their first question.

00:21:10.570 --> 00:21:12.486
You've already talked
about some of the things

00:21:12.486 --> 00:21:13.750
you're using in GCP.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:16.790
Obviously, you're running
on Compute Engine.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:19.329
Big Table is obviously a big
part of the architecture.

00:21:19.329 --> 00:21:20.912
And you've already
told us that you're

00:21:20.912 --> 00:21:23.657
using BigQuery for analysis.

00:21:23.657 --> 00:21:25.490
I realize it may be
somewhat difficult to do

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:27.114
without a visual aid,
but I'm wondering

00:21:27.114 --> 00:21:29.800
if you can give us a
short summary of what

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:32.690
does your architecture
on GCP look like

00:21:32.690 --> 00:21:37.049
and what products in GCP
are you really using?

00:21:37.049 --> 00:21:38.340
DAVID MYTTON: Yeah, absolutely.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:41.380
So the first product that
we started using, as I said,

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:42.730
was Big Table.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:46.230
And when we moved that use
case over, we were at the point

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:49.080
where we needed to rewrite
that project anyway.

00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:52.080
So we were lucky to be able to
spend the time to completely

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:55.530
rewrite that from scratch
to use Container Engine,

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:57.510
to have it completely
containerized,

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:01.210
and to use all of the core
Google products around it.

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:05.010
But with the other parts of the
application that we moved over,

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:08.040
we decided we wanted to get onto
Google as quickly as we could,

00:22:08.040 --> 00:22:10.140
and so we just
lifted them directly

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:13.260
from VMs or the
bare metal servers

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:17.340
and they run completely onto
Compute Engine instances.

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:19.252
So for many of the
systems that we have,

00:22:19.252 --> 00:22:21.460
we're not getting the full
advantage of Google Cloud,

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:23.460
in the sense we're not
using all of the products

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:24.930
that we might use.

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:27.480
But that's the direction
that we're going.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:32.190
And so the direct lift from
the VMs and the bare metal

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:34.140
over to the Compute
Engine instances

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:36.130
was aided because
the architectures

00:22:36.130 --> 00:22:38.250
is a microservices setup.

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:42.300
So we have probably 15 or
20 or so different services

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:45.270
internally within
Server Density that

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:47.680
manage different areas
of the application.

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:52.240
And these are all made available
internally through an HTTP API.

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:54.150
And our web UI for
customers is built

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:56.760
on top of that with an
authentication layer,

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:58.740
as is our public API.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:01.830
So as a Server Density customer,
if you are using our API,

00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:04.260
you're using the same API
that our engineers are

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:07.170
using internally to manage
the different aspects

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:08.160
of your account.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:11.950
So things like alert
configurations, user profiles,

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:14.640
the inventory in your account
for the different resources

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:18.840
you're monitoring, these
are all based around APIs.

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:22.620
And the architecture
itself is microservices,

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:24.510
and the code is mostly Python.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:28.050
So we have load balances, the
Google load balance products

00:23:28.050 --> 00:23:29.400
internally.

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:32.160
We also use NGINX in a
couple of different locations

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:34.136
and where we're running
our own load balances.

00:23:34.136 --> 00:23:35.760
But our goal is to
move everything over

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:39.360
to Google services so that we
can containerize everything

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:42.700
and also take advantage
of things like the Pub/Sub

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:46.435
product, which will be
replace-- our plan is to replace

00:23:46.435 --> 00:23:49.290
the Kafka and Storm
setup we have,

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.390
so when alert
payloads come in, they

00:23:51.390 --> 00:23:54.554
get processed through Kafka
as the queueing system.

00:23:54.554 --> 00:23:56.470
And then we have a number
of different engines

00:23:56.470 --> 00:23:59.370
which determine whether an
alert should be triggered

00:23:59.370 --> 00:24:00.880
and where the data will go.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:04.065
So part of it will go into
the alert engine, part of it

00:24:04.065 --> 00:24:07.865
will go into Big Table to be
stored for long-term storage.

00:24:07.865 --> 00:24:10.860
And so the plan is to
replace Kafka with Pub/Sub

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:13.320
at some point, so that we
get the full managed service

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:14.780
element of it.

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:19.605
And so the architecture
is fairly sophisticated.

00:24:19.605 --> 00:24:20.720
YURI: Thank you very much.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:21.930
And that actually,
I think, starts

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:23.430
to answer one of
the other questions

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:26.820
that we have on the line here,
which is, how should folks

00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:29.160
think about migrating to GCP?

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:31.710
Should they be, like
you said, trying

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:33.450
to get to Google
as fast as possible

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:37.020
by simply migrating their
VMs to Compute Engine?

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:38.910
Should they be
re-architecting and re-writing

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:41.650
their applications or
just containerizing them?

00:24:41.650 --> 00:24:45.161
And it sounds like the path you
chose is some of all of that,

00:24:45.161 --> 00:24:45.660
right?

00:24:45.660 --> 00:24:48.334
Some things you
migrated as they were.

00:24:48.334 --> 00:24:50.250
Some things you ended
up rewriting or at least

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:52.810
containerizing.

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:54.494
Based on your
experience with that,

00:24:54.494 --> 00:24:56.410
do you think you would
do anything differently

00:24:56.410 --> 00:24:57.540
if you were to do it again?

00:24:57.540 --> 00:25:01.360
Any kind of lessons learned
from some of those choices?

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:04.680
DAVID MYTTON: I think the
underlying principle should

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:06.690
be to keep it as
simple as possible.

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:10.560
Because doing a migration to
a new provider is a big task,

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:12.130
and it's going to
take a lot of time.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:15.340
And you want to have as few
moving parts as possible.

00:25:15.340 --> 00:25:19.320
So our initial workload
was to rewrite and deploy

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:23.660
onto Big Table, using all of
the Google products around that.

00:25:23.660 --> 00:25:24.960
But we had no time constraints.

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:27.570
Because this was the first
thing, we were testing it out.

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:30.420
And we connected Google up
to our previous environment

00:25:30.420 --> 00:25:33.270
using a secure VPN.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:34.600
And that has its overheads.

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:36.790
There's obviously
a bandwidth limit.

00:25:36.790 --> 00:25:39.504
There's a cost to sending
data over the internet.

00:25:39.504 --> 00:25:40.920
Although it's
encrypted, you still

00:25:40.920 --> 00:25:43.140
have to pay for all
the data transfer.

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:46.240
And there is a small
performance hit because you're

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:50.280
moving data over the internet.

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:53.910
But really, moving
the resources should

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:55.680
be done as quickly
as you possibly can,

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:57.450
because of the
complexity of doing it.

00:25:57.450 --> 00:26:00.690
And if you're going to rewrite
the entire architecture

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:03.360
at the same time, you're
going to end up changing

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:04.450
so many different things.

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:08.112
It's probably going
to cause problems.

00:26:08.112 --> 00:26:08.830
YURI: Excellent.

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:10.750
Thank you so much.

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:14.860
And then I'll ask you one
last question, which I

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:16.390
know may not be an easy answer.

00:26:19.219 --> 00:26:21.010
Perhaps you answered
this at the beginning.

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:23.200
But if you were to
summarize your decision

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:26.560
to choose GCP over
AWS, what would

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:30.420
you say were the top
two or three things that

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:31.935
swayed your decision?

00:26:31.935 --> 00:26:33.940
And also, I would love
to hear, personally,

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:35.100
if there was anything
that actually made

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:36.683
that decision
difficult, if there were

00:26:36.683 --> 00:26:39.410
some advantages that AWS
had that you essentially

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:42.230
had to trade off in
order to take advantage

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:44.390
of the benefits of GCP.

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:46.470
DAVID MYTTON: So I'll
start with the second bit,

00:26:46.470 --> 00:26:49.200
because I think it's probably
the most obvious one, which is

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:51.770
that AWS is the biggest Cloud.

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:54.490
And so there are more
people who know about AWS.

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:56.000
There's more blog posts.

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.240
There's more resources.

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:00.950
They have just a
bigger ecosystem.

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:03.960
And that can make a
difficult decision

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:06.750
to go with what I think is
actually a better technology

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:07.830
choice.

00:27:07.830 --> 00:27:11.970
Because although Google doesn't
have as many products, when

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:15.240
it comes to comparing the
portfolios against AWS,

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.070
the products that
are there, I think,

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:21.210
are much better designed
and much better implemented.

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:22.820
Just the documentation
is better.

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:23.940
The APIs are better.

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:25.330
The interface is better.

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:27.550
It's much more consistent.

00:27:27.550 --> 00:27:29.770
And although some
features might be missing,

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:32.690
the ones that are there are the
ones you actually need to use.

00:27:32.690 --> 00:27:36.150
And they just work as
you'd expect it to.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:39.900
And it's clearly been designed
from the beginning, which

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:41.970
everything, I suppose,
has iterations.

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:44.010
And AWS has the
advantage and also

00:27:44.010 --> 00:27:46.840
disadvantage of having been
around for a very long time.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:48.960
So they've got
legacy things that

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:52.350
don't work quite so consistently
or aren't quite as well

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:55.950
designed, just because
they've been built on later.

00:27:55.950 --> 00:28:00.120
Whereas GCP has the advantage
of all of Google's experience

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:03.450
being able to go into it
from the beginning as a newer

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:04.870
product.

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:08.190
I think the thing that probably
stood out the most for us

00:28:08.190 --> 00:28:10.530
and where we had to make
changes after migrating

00:28:10.530 --> 00:28:13.500
was around the networking
and the cost of that.

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:15.180
We moved from an
environment where

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:17.805
we didn't have to pay for any
internal networking at all,

00:28:17.805 --> 00:28:21.030
all the traffic was completely
free across all of the data

00:28:21.030 --> 00:28:22.167
centers that we had.

00:28:22.167 --> 00:28:24.000
And when you move to
the Cloud, whether it's

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:25.830
Google or any other
Cloud provider,

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:29.400
they all charge for all
of the network usage

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:31.140
outside of a single zone.

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:33.660
And for the best
practices, you have

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:35.800
to deploy across multiple zones.

00:28:35.800 --> 00:28:38.030
And you need to do that
just to get the redundancy

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:41.490
and reliability, and ideally
across multiple regions.

00:28:41.490 --> 00:28:43.420
But it's really
expensive to do that,

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:46.020
particularly if you have noisy
services internally that you

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:49.380
don't control, so something
like Kafka and sending the data

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:51.120
around different clusters.

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:53.940
Or even database replication
can be very noisy.

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:55.970
And you have no real
control over that.

00:28:55.970 --> 00:29:00.900
And it can be a hidden cost
that you might not be expecting.

00:29:00.900 --> 00:29:02.444
NICK ORLOVE: That makes sense.

00:29:02.444 --> 00:29:04.610
And I know that there's
quite a few papers out there

00:29:04.610 --> 00:29:07.040
that Google's published, that
the industry has published

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:11.300
on trying to rationalize
building for best practices,

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:13.640
building for high
availability versus building

00:29:13.640 --> 00:29:14.850
for cost optimization.

00:29:14.850 --> 00:29:17.460
So it absolutely makes sense.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:20.150
So I know that we're
right around the mark.

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:22.220
So maybe if I can ask
one last question,

00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:27.230
maybe the $1,000 question is did
you find that it was worth it?

00:29:27.230 --> 00:29:29.480
Are you glad that
you moved over?

00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:34.519
Any last thoughts that you
want to share on that subject?

00:29:34.519 --> 00:29:35.810
DAVID MYTTON: Yeah, absolutely.

00:29:35.810 --> 00:29:38.240
We are very happy
now that we've moved

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:40.340
onto modern infrastructure.

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:43.220
And we can let Google
deal with some things that

00:29:43.220 --> 00:29:45.600
just have no
additional value for us

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.900
as a competitive differentiator.

00:29:47.900 --> 00:29:49.400
So scaling the time
series database,

00:29:49.400 --> 00:29:51.800
we don't have to deal
with that anymore.

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:56.594
Our overall cost has been
reduced by almost 50%.

00:29:56.594 --> 00:29:58.010
And that's because
we've been able

00:29:58.010 --> 00:30:02.150
to get significant efficiencies
from using a managed service as

00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:04.624
opposed to having really
beefy hardware, which

00:30:04.624 --> 00:30:06.540
might have been fun to
say you've got hundreds

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:11.000
and thousands of gigabytes
and terabytes of SSD,

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:15.060
but actually wasn't delivering
enough value for the business.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:18.170
We can also say we're on
modern infrastructure that

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:22.310
is carbon neutral and moving
towards being completely

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:24.870
renewable, which is great
for us as a business.

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:28.640
But our engineers love
being in an environment that

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:32.609
has modern APIs and modern
systems and features.

00:30:32.609 --> 00:30:34.150
And so it's definitely
been worth it.

00:30:34.150 --> 00:30:36.816
And yeah, I'd highly
recommend the move.

00:30:36.816 --> 00:30:37.580
NICK ORLOVE: Cool.

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:40.021
Well, thank you very much
for all the feedback.

00:30:40.021 --> 00:30:41.770
And actually, I do
have one more question.

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:43.750
Since you are our
customer, since you

00:30:43.750 --> 00:30:49.089
guys at Server Density are
having some amazing products

00:30:49.089 --> 00:30:50.880
that are helping our
customers, helping us,

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:54.760
et cetera, anything in 2018
that we can look forward to

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:56.780
from a Server
Density perspective?

00:30:56.780 --> 00:30:58.440
Anything cool coming
down the road?

00:30:58.440 --> 00:31:01.540
DAVID MYTTON: So the main
thing is the integrations

00:31:01.540 --> 00:31:04.110
that we have with
various services

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:05.110
that people are running.

00:31:05.110 --> 00:31:07.324
So whether that's software,
databases, web servers,

00:31:07.324 --> 00:31:09.490
anything that you're running
in your infrastructure,

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:12.300
and also the Cloud providers
that you might be using,

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:14.650
we're building significantly
better integrations

00:31:14.650 --> 00:31:17.980
that we can pull in data
from any of your environments

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:20.440
and help you visualize
and monitor it,

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:23.900
and making sure that that is
visualized in interesting ways.

00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:24.610
Graphs are cool.

00:31:24.610 --> 00:31:28.680
But there are other ways
to get the information out

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:30.370
of your infrastructure
about what's

00:31:30.370 --> 00:31:33.620
going on, where the problems
are, and how to optimize it.

00:31:33.620 --> 00:31:35.610
So that's what we're building.

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:36.580
NICK ORLOVE: Cool.

00:31:36.580 --> 00:31:37.536
That's fantastic.

00:31:37.536 --> 00:31:38.910
Well, David, I
feel like we could

00:31:38.910 --> 00:31:41.260
talk to you for so much longer.

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:43.185
I just love all the
insights that you have.

00:31:43.185 --> 00:31:44.650
You've walked this path before.

00:31:44.650 --> 00:31:47.020
So thank you very much for
spending the time, especially

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:50.110
since it's after hours
in the UK right now.

00:31:50.110 --> 00:31:51.920
But to everyone else
who's listening,

00:31:51.920 --> 00:31:54.860
thank you for watching.

00:31:54.860 --> 00:31:58.920
Please remember to rate, thumbs
up, thumbs down, subscribe.

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:00.670
Yeah, hopefully thumbs up.

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:03.040
And we will see you next time.

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:03.939
So, awesome.

00:32:03.939 --> 00:32:04.980
Thank you so much, David.

00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:05.590
Thank you, Yuri.

00:32:05.590 --> 00:32:06.730
YURI: Thanks for having me.

00:32:06.730 --> 00:32:07.440
NICK ORLOVE: And thank
you everyone out there.

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:08.440
DAVID MYTTON: Thank you.

00:32:08.440 --> 00:32:09.959
NICK ORLOVE: See you later.

